  "query_preview": "..Generally benign conditions are expected for the\nfirst half of this week. While a couple weak systems are expected\nto brush northern Utah through mid-week -- the first moving \nthrough overnight tonight, and the second late Tuesday into \nWednesday -- little impact is expected outside of a slight \ncooldown and some isolated showers near the Idaho border.\n\nA more active pattern is expected late this week into the weekend\nas a couple stronger weather systems with more efficient moisture\ntaps impact the region. The first system arrives Friday as a \nshortwave ejects around the eastern periphery of a deepening \ntrough along the west coast. Increasing southerly flow aloft will \nbring an influx of tropical moisture to the region, with PWAT \nanomalies increasing to around 200-250% of normal across much of \nsouthern and central Utah on Friday into early Saturday. There is \nstill quite a bit of spread on how much precipitation this system \nbrings to the region, with the 25th percentile showing next to \nnorthing across much of Utah and southwest Wyoming, while the 75th\npercentile brings 0.3-0.5 inches to much of southern Utah except \nupwards of an inch across the southern mountains. Given recent \nrainfall across central and southern Utah, the flash flood risk is\nelevated Friday into Saturday despite this uncertainty. WPC \ncurrently highlights much of southern Utah under a Marginal Risk \n(Level 1 of 4) of excessive rainfall through this period.\n\nEnsembles show good agreement on a brief lull on Saturday \nafternoon ahead of the second, more potent system. A strong \natmospheric river is expected to make landfall along the southern \nCalifornia coast late Saturday, bringing a stream of very deep \ntropical moisture that will overspread much of Utah and southwest \nWyoming on Sunday. Concurrently, an upper-level trough begins to \ndig southward into the Great Basin, resulting in substantial \ninstability developing across the region. As such, ensembles are \nin good agreement on this portion of the storm bringing widespread\nmoderate to heavy rainfall to the region. While heavy rain is \nlikely across southern Utah, with a 75% chance to exceed 1\" of \nrain across much of the area through Monday morning, models show \nquite a bit of spread in rainfall accumulations across central and\nnorthern Utah given some models indicate potential for a more \nsoutherly track of deepest moisture. Across these areas, the 25th \npercentile brings a widespread 0.2-0.5\", while the 75th percentile\nindicates widespread accumulations of 1.0-1.5\". \n\nCooler air moving in with the aforementioned trough on Sunday \nwill result in snow levels dipping around 8000-8500 feet. As such,\naccumulating snowfall is expected for all Utah mountains into \nMonday morning. Current forecast indicates 3-6\" of snow \naccumulations between Sunday and Monday morning for elevations \nabove 8500 feet, with locally higher totals of 6-10\" across the \ncentral and southern Utah mountains and the Uintas.",
